 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; NODE-LINK PATTERNS, VERSIONED (claude-1 ruling, 2026-09-06, on PA2z's
 ;; blind-spot finding). PA2z measured that v1 misses node linkage expressed as
 ;; a FUNCTION-CALL ARGUMENT and so cannot see nine route-tagged nodes. The
 ;; ruling was: widen the pattern rather than have the lane's operator read call
 ;; sites by hand, because "a harness known to under-report, patched per-slice by
 ;; the operator reading call sites, is precisely the hand-census failure mode
 ;; PA1z was built to remove -- manual discrimination as standing policy would
 ;; make you the instrument again."
 ;;
 ;; Both versions are kept. v1 is what ALIGN's 42 cells were measured under and
 ;; is never edited; comparability is preserved by MEASUREMENT (re-run the 42
 ;; under v2 and show a zero-verdict diff) rather than by freezing a known blind
 ;; spot.
 :pattern-version :v2
 :node-link-patterns
 {:v1 {:forms ["(:node|:wm/node|:route/node|:control-node)[[:space:]]+:%s"]
       :scope-key :node-link
       :provenance "Lifted verbatim from ALIGN section 1. The 42 adjudicated cells were measured under this and under this scope."
       :known-limit "Matches only a LITERAL map entry. Blind to a node keyword passed as a call argument."}
  :v2 {:forms ["(:node|:wm/node|:route/node|:control-node)[[:space:]]+:%s"
               "route-tag[^)]*:%s[^0-9A-Za-z-]"]
       :scope-key :v2-node-link
       :provenance "v1's form plus the constructor-call form PA2z measured."
       :form-note "No backslash escapes: EDN rejects a \\( escape inside a string, and the right boundary is expressed as [^0-9A-Za-z-] rather than a word boundary so :R1 cannot match :R12. Every route-tag call site has a space and a via-string after the node keyword, so the trailing class always has something to match."
       :constructors-are-data
       "The second form names its constructors EXPLICITLY rather than matching any call with a node keyword in it, which would match comments and unrelated maps and over-report. The constructor set was derived mechanically, not guessed: `rg -n ':node[[:space:]]+[a-z][a-z0-9-]*'` across the whole declared scope returns the {:node <param>} shape in four files, and only war_machine.clj:5868 (route-tag) binds a CONTROL-STAGE node that way -- hierarchical_budget.clj uses :node for a hierarchy node, a different concept, and full_loop_runner.clj:225-226 READS (:node from) rather than constructing a link. CORRECTION (2026-09-06, PA3z): an earlier version of this note said node_sim.clj also used :node for a different concept. That was too strong and is wrong -- node_sim.clj:327 genuinely carries {:node :R5 ...} for the control-stage R5. It does not affect the constructor derivation (node_sim defines no node-linking constructor; :327 is a literal map that v1's own form already catches), but the reason as stated was inaccurate and is corrected rather than quietly dropped. What is true of node_sim is different and is recorded in :known-false-positive-class below: it is a per-node SIMULATION harness that runs one node in isolation -- its own header states that no live tick is taken, no run lock, nothing written under the data directory (node_sim.clj:1-12), so its node linkage is a simulation declaration and not lifecycle conduct. A new constructor is added here with its evidence, the same discipline as the adjudication ledger."
       :scope-note "v2 searches the union of the v1 node-link paths and the paths ALIGN ALREADY DECLARED IT READ (:also-read). This is NOT a widening into new territory -- futon3 stays outside per claude-1's scope ruling of 2026-09-06 -- it is searching the files ALIGN's own section 1 says it read, with a pattern that can now see what is in them."}}

