 ;; :serves-cells DECLARATIONS (PA15z, mechanism (a)). Lifted from ALIGN --
 ;; futon2 2a495a7d (R10) and e53f1e26 (R12/R20/R16). NOT restated: the reading
 ;; is claude-1's, this is the instrument consuming it.
 ;;
 ;; THE DEFECT THESE FIX. The node-link search establishes NODE linkage; the
 ;; census reads CELL verdicts. While every node was fully adjudicated or wholly
 ;; unlinked the difference never showed. The moment PA6z gave R10 three real
 ;; :node :R10 sites, the five cells no adjudication claimed found the SAME
 ;; three hits and reported :hit-needs-adjudication -- correct behaviour, useless
 ;; answer, census exit 4.
 ;;
 ;; MATCHING IS BY SITE, NOT BY NODE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. A hit is
 ;; ACCOUNTED FOR when it falls inside a declared site range. A cell not named in
 ;; any :serves-cells is :absent ONLY IF EVERY HIT IT FOUND IS ACCOUNTED FOR. A
 ;; hit at an UNDECLARED site is new conduct the census has never adjudicated,
 ;; and it still reports :hit-needs-adjudication.
 ;;
 ;; Node-wide silencing was the obvious implementation and it is the wrong one:
 ;; it would mark every unclaimed cell absent forever, so the next R10 boundary
 ;; anyone builds would be invisible to the instrument that exists to find it.
 ;; That is PA2z's blindness with the sign flipped, and PA15z's acceptance
 ;; demands a control for BOTH directions precisely so this cannot be shipped by
 ;; accident.
 ;;
 ;; WHY THE TIDIER RULE WAS REFUSED. PA6z's evidence carries
 ;; :process/stage :dispatched, and a grep across the whole declared scope
 ;; returns exactly ONE occurrence -- so "read the code's own stage tag" looks
 ;; like a cleaner mechanical discriminator than a ledger declaration. It is
 ;; unsafe alone, and ALIGN records the counterexample inside the adjudication:
 ;; R10 :commissioned is credited on the :r10/invalid-commission refusal, which
 ;; carries NO stage tag. A tag-only rule would uncredit a genuinely established
 ;; cell. So the declaration decides and the stage tag corroborates.
 :serves-cells-declarations
